00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.620
In the previous video, you saw the logistic regression model.

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.740
To train the parameters w and b of your logistic regression model, you need to define a cost

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:10.740
function.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:14.480
Let's take a look at the cost function you can use to train logistic regression.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:18.320
To recap, this is what we had defined from the previous slide.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:24.300
So your output y hat is sigmoid of w transpose x plus b, where sigmoid of z is as defined

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:25.300
here.

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:30.860
To learn parameters for your model, you're given a training set of m training examples,

00:00:30.860 --> 00:00:35.700
and it seems natural that you want to find parameters w and b so that at least on the

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.380
training set, the output you have, the predictions you have on the training set, which I'm going

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:46.040
to write as y hat i, that that would be close to the ground truth labels yi that you

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.300
got in the training set.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.980
So to fill in a little bit more detail for the equation on top, we had said that y hat

00:00:53.980 --> 00:01:01.020
is as defined at the top for a training example x, and of course for each training example,

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:06.260
we're using these superscripts with round brackets with parentheses to index into different

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:07.940
training examples.

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:14.780
Your prediction on training example i, which is y hat i, is going to be obtained by taking

00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:20.140
the sigmoid function and applying it to w transpose xi, the input for the training

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:21.500
example plus b.

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:30.300
And you can also define zi as follows, where zi is equal to w transpose xi plus b.

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.780
So throughout this course, we're going to use this notational convention that the superscript

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:45.500
parentheses i refers to data, be it x or y or z or something else, associated with the

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:51.180
i-th training example, associated with the i-th example.

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:54.940
That's what the superscript i in parentheses means.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:02:00.260
Now let's see what loss function or an error function we can use to measure how well our

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.460
algorithm is doing.

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:07.780
One thing you could do is define the loss when your algorithm outputs y hat and the true

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:12.420
label is y to be maybe the squared error or one half a squared error.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:17.340
It turns out that you could do this, but in logistic regression, people don't usually

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.500
do this because when you come to learn the parameters, you find that the optimization

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.740
problem, which we'll talk about later, becomes non-convex.

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:30.300
So you end up with an optimization problem with multiple local optima.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:33.500
So gradient descent may not find a global optimum.

00:02:33.500 --> 00:02:36.460
If you didn't understand the last couple of comments, don't worry about it.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:38.500
We'll get to it in a later video.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:43.940
But the intuition to take away is that this function, L, called the loss function, is a

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:50.460
function we'll need to define to measure how good our output y hat is when the true label

00:02:50.460 --> 00:02:51.460
is y.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:55.940
And squared error seems like it might be a reasonable choice, except that it makes gradient

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:58.280
descent not work well.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:03.100
So in logistic regression, we will actually define a different loss function that plays

00:03:03.100 --> 00:03:09.620
a similar role as squared error, but will give us an optimization problem that is convex.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:13.900
And so we'll see in a later video, becomes much easier to optimize.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:20.500
So what we use in logistic regression is actually the following loss function, which I'm just

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:35.410
going to write out here, is negative y log y hat plus 1 minus y log 1 minus y hat.

00:03:35.410 --> 00:03:38.930
Here's some intuition for why this loss function makes sense.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:44.650
Keep in mind that if we're using squared error, then you want the squared error to be as

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:46.010
small as possible.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:51.610
And with this logistic regression, loss function will also want this to be as small as possible.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:55.430
To understand why this makes sense, let's look at the two cases.

00:03:55.430 --> 00:04:03.110
In the first case, let's say y is equal to 1, then the loss function y hat comma y is

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.490
just this first term, this negative sign.

00:04:05.490 --> 00:04:10.090
So it's negative log y hat if y is equal to 1.

00:04:10.090 --> 00:04:14.210
Because if y equals 1, then the second term 1 minus y is equal to 0.

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:20.390
So this says if y equals 1, you want negative log y hat to be as small as possible.

00:04:20.390 --> 00:04:29.050
So that means you want log y hat to be large, to be as big as possible.

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:33.510
And that means you want y hat to be large.

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:39.030
But because y hat is the sigmoid function, it can never be bigger than 1.

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:44.630
So this is saying that if y is equal to 1, you want y hat to be as big as possible, but

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.030
it can't ever be bigger than 1.

00:04:46.030 --> 00:04:48.730
So saying you want y hat to be close to 1 as well.

00:04:48.730 --> 00:04:54.890
The other case is if y equals 0, then this first term in the loss function is equal to

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:57.570
0 because y is 0.

00:04:57.570 --> 00:05:01.830
And then the second term defines the loss function.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:07.730
So the loss becomes negative log 1 minus y hat.

00:05:07.730 --> 00:05:12.090
And so if in your learning procedure you try to make the loss function small, what

00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:21.900
this means is that you want log 1 minus y hat to be large because there's a negative sign

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:22.900
there.

00:05:22.900 --> 00:05:27.020
And then through a similar piece of reasoning, you can conclude that this loss function is

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:31.380
trying to make y hat as small as possible.

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:36.860
And again, because y hat has to be between 0 and 1, this is saying that if y is equal

00:05:36.860 --> 00:05:43.340
to 0, then your loss function will push the parameters to make y hat as close to 0 as

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:44.340
possible.

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:49.540
Now, there are a lot of functions with roughly this effect that if y is equal to 1, you try

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:53.420
to make y hat large, and y is equal to 0, you try to make y hat small.

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:59.780
We just gave here in green a somewhat informal justification for this particular loss function.

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:04.900
We'll provide an optional video later to give a more formal justification for y in

00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:09.020
logistic regression, we like to use the loss function with this particular form.

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:14.060
Finally, the loss function was defined with respect to a single training example.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.820
It measures how well you're doing on a single training example.

00:06:16.820 --> 00:06:23.300
I'm now going to define something called the cost function, which measures how well you're

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:25.180
doing on the entire training set.

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:32.060
So the cost function, j, which is applied to your parameters w and b, is going to be

00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:33.340
the average.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:43.030
One over m of the sum of the loss function applied to each of the training examples in

00:06:43.030 --> 00:06:49.150
turn, where here y hat is of course the prediction output by your logistic regression algorithm

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:53.110
using a particular set of parameters w and b.

00:06:53.110 --> 00:07:00.190
And so just to expand this out, this is equal to negative one over m sum from i equals

00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:04.070
one through m of the definition of the loss function above.

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:16.470
So this is yi log y hat i plus one minus yi log one minus y hat i.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:18.390
I guess I can put square brackets here.

00:07:18.390 --> 00:07:21.550
So the minus sign is outside everything else.

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:27.070
So the terminology I'm going to use is that the loss function is applied to just a single

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:29.750
training example like so.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:33.630
And the cost function is the cost of your parameters.

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:38.950
So in training your logistic regression model, we're going to try to find parameters w and

00:07:38.950 --> 00:07:44.070
b that minimize the overall cost function j written at the bottom.

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:49.710
So you've just seen the setup for the logistic regression algorithm, the loss function for

00:07:49.710 --> 00:07:54.690
training example, and the overall cost function for the parameters of your algorithm.

00:07:54.690 --> 00:08:00.110
It turns out that logistic regression can be viewed as a very, very small neural network.

